  "output": "Now I have a full picture. Let me write up the findings.\n\n---\n\n## Performance Analysis: HR Policy Bot Proto\n\nThis is a Python/FastAPI backend — no React. The frontend is static files, so \"re-renders\" don't apply. The real performance issues are in DB connection patterns, concurrency bottlenecks, redundant computation, and missing in-process caching.\n\n---\n\n### 1. N+1 Database Connection Pattern (Critical)\n\n**Location:** `src/data/db.py` — every method\n\nEvery single DB operation opens and tears down an `aiosqlite` connection. A single query flow triggers at least 5 separate `aiosqlite.connect()` calls: `save_query`, `get_cached` (per URL, N times across specialists), `set_cached` (per URL), `save_orchestrator_response`, then `save_query` again.\n\n```python\n# Current — opens a new connection every call:\nasync def save_query(self, record: QueryRecord) -> None:\n    async with aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path) as db:\n        await db.execute(...)\n        await db.commit()\n\nasync def get_agent_responses(self, query_id: str) -> list[AgentResponseRecord]:\n    async with aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path) as db:  # another new connection\n        ...\n```\n\n**Fix:** Hold a persistent connection, opened once at startup and closed at shutdown:\n\n```python\nclass DatabaseManager:\n    def __init__(self, db_path: Path = _DB_PATH) -> None:\n        self._db_path = db_path\n        self._db_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        self._db: aiosqlite.Connection | None = None\n\n    async def initialize(self) -> None:\n        self._db = await aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path)\n        self._db.row_factory = aiosqlite.Row\n        await self._db.executescript(_SCHEMA)\n        await self._db.commit()\n\n    async def close(self) -> None:\n        if self._db:\n            await self._db.close()\n\n    async def save_query(self, record: QueryRecord) -> None:\n        await self._db.execute(\"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO queries ...\", (...))\n        await self._db.commit()\n```\n\nWire `initialize()` and `close()` into FastAPI's `lifespan` alongside the existing DB init.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Global Domain Lock Held During Sleep (Critical)\n\n**Location:** `src/fetch/engine.py:79-84`\n\n`_domain_lock` is a single `asyncio.Lock` acquired for every domain rate-limit check. Crucially, `await asyncio.sleep(delay_required)` happens **while holding the lock**, which serializes all concurrent fetches to different domains behind one waiter.\n\n```python\n# Current — all fetches queue behind one lock while sleeping:\nasync with self._domain_lock:\n    last_request = self._domain_last_request[domain]\n    delay_required = 1.0 - (time.time() - last_request)\n    if delay_required > 0:\n        await asyncio.sleep(delay_required)  # blocks the lock!\n    self._domain_last_request[domain] = time.time()\n```\n\n**Fix:** Use per-domain locks, and release the global lock before sleeping:\n\n```python\n_domain_locks: dict[str, asyncio.Lock] = defaultdict(asyncio.Lock)\n\nasync def _fetch_with_concurrency(self, url: str) -> str:\n    domain = self._get_domain(url)\n    async with self._domain_locks[domain]:\n        last_request = self._domain_last_request[domain]\n        delay_required = 1.0 - (time.time() - last_request)\n        if delay_required > 0:\n            await asyncio.sleep(delay_required)\n        self._domain_last_request[domain] = time.time()\n    async with self._domain_semaphores[domain]:\n        return await self._do_fetch(url)\n```\n\nThis allows fetches to different domains to proceed concurrently without waiting on each other's rate-limit delay.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Regex Recompiled on Every Fetch\n\n**Location:** `src/fetch/engine.py:68-72`\n\n```python\ndef _normalize_url(self, url: str) -> str:\n    import re\n    if re.search(r'laws-lois\\.justice\\.gc\\.ca/eng/acts/[^/]+/$', url):\n        ...\n```\n\n`re.search()` with a raw pattern string recompiles the regex on every call. The `import re` inside the method adds an attribute lookup on each invocation too.\n\n**Fix:** Pre-compile at module level:\n\n```python\nimport re\n\n_LAWS_ACT_RE = re.compile(r'laws-lois\\.justice\\.gc\\.ca/eng/acts/[^/]+/$')\n\nclass FetchEngine:\n    def _normalize_url(self, url: str) -> str:\n        if _LAWS_ACT_RE.search(url):\n            return url.rstrip('/') + '/FullText.html'\n        return url\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Policy Registry JSON Read 8 Times at Startup\n\n**Location:** `src/agents/specialist.py:72-75`\n\n```python\ndef _load_instruments(self, agent_id: str) -> list[dict]:\n    with open(_REGISTRY_PATH) as f:\n        registry = json.load(f)           # reads and parses JSON for EACH of 8 agents\n    return [r for r in registry if agent_id in r[\"agent_ids\"]]\n```\n\n`get_specialists()` creates 8 `SpecialistAgent` instances, each opening and parsing the same JSON file.\n\n**Fix:** Load once at module level:\n\n```python\ndef _load_registry() -> list[dict]:\n    with open(_REGISTRY_PATH) as f:\n        return json.load(f)\n\n_REGISTRY: list[dict] = _load_registry()\n\nclass SpecialistAgent(GenericAgent):\n    def _load_instruments(self, agent_id: str) -> list[dict]:\n        return [r for r in _REGISTRY if agent_id in r[\"agent_ids\"]]\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. `GenerativeModel` Recreated on Every LLM Call\n\n**Location:** `src/llm/client.py:40-50`, called from `chat()`, `stream_chat()`, `chat_parsed()`\n\n```python\nasync def chat(self, system_prompt: str, user_message: str, model: str | None = None) -> str:\n    m = self._make_model(model)   # creates a new GenerativeModel object each call\n    ...\n```\n\n`_make_model()` constructs a new `genai.GenerativeModel` with a `GenerationConfig` dict on every request. The model name and temperature don't change between calls.\n\n**Fix:** Cache models by `(model_name, json_output)`:\n\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\nclass GeminiClient:\n    @lru_cache(maxsize=4)\n    def _get_model(self, model_name: str, json_output: bool) -> genai.GenerativeModel:\n        generation_config: dict = {\n            \"temperature\": self._config.model.temperature,\n            \"top_p\": self._config.model.top_p,\n        }\n        if json_output:\n            generation_config[\"response_mime_type\"] = \"application/json\"\n        return genai.GenerativeModel(\n            model_name=model_name,\n            generation_config=generation_config,\n        )\n\n    async def chat(self, system_prompt: str, user_message: str, model: str | None = None) -> str:\n        m = self._get_model(model or self._model_name, False)\n        ...\n```\n\n---\n\n### 6. `content_hash` SHA-256 Recomputed on Every Access\n\n**Location:** `src/fetch/cache.py:37-39`\n\n```python\n@property\ndef content_hash(self) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(self.content.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()\n```\n\nThis is called once per `set_cached()` call, but it hashes potentially 24KB+ of policy text every time. If `content_hash` is ever called more than once (e.g. during debugging, logging, or future changes), it recomputes.\n\n**Fix:** Cache it:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass CacheEntry:\n    url: str\n    content: str\n    ttl_hours: int\n    status_code: int | None = None\n    content_length: int | None = None\n    _content_hash: str | None = field(default=None, repr=False)\n\n    @property\n    def content_hash(self) -> str:\n        if self._content_hash is None:\n            self._content_hash = hashlib.sha256(self.content.encode()).hexdigest()\n        return self._content_hash\n```\n\n---\n\n### 7. `save_query` Called Twice Per Request\n\n**Location:** `src/api/websocket.py:162`, then `182`\n\n```python\nquery_record = QueryRecord(query_id=query_id, query_text=query_text, agents_invoked=[])\nawait db_manager.save_query(query_record)      # first write — incomplete record\n\n# ... all processing ...\n\nquery_record.processing_time_ms = int(processing_time)\nquery_record.agents_invoked = response.agents_consulted\nawait db_manager.save_query(query_record)      # second write via INSERT OR REPLACE\n```\n\nThe first write stores a record with empty `agents_invoked` purely so something exists in the DB before processing. The second write replaces it. This is two full round-trips for one logical record.\n\n**Fix:** Only write once, after processing completes. If crash recovery is a concern, save the in-flight query to an in-memory dict instead:\n\n```python\n# Remove the pre-processing save_query call.\n# Build the complete record after orchestrator returns:\nquery_record = QueryRecord(\n    query_id=query_id,\n    query_text=query_text,\n    agents_invoked=response.agents_consulted,\n    overall_confidence=response.overall_confidence,\n    processing_time_ms=int(processing_time),\n)\nawait db_manager.save_query(query_record)\n```\n\n---\n\n### Priority Summary\n\n| # | Issue | Effort | Impact |\n|---|-------|--------|--------|\n| 2 | Global domain lock held during sleep | Low | High — unblocks parallel specialist fetches |\n| 1 | N+1 DB connections | Medium | High — eliminates connection overhead on every operation |\n| 4 | Registry JSON read 8× at startup | Low | Medium — one-time startup cost, easy win |\n| 5 | `GenerativeModel` recreated per call | Low | Medium — object construction + config allocation |\n| 3 | Regex recompiled per fetch | Trivial | Low — micro-optimization, but correct practice |\n| 6 | SHA-256 recomputed per access | Trivial | Low — only matters if `content_hash` called repeatedly |\n| 7 | Double `save_query` write | Low | Low — one redundant DB round-trip per query |\n\nItems 2 and 1 will have the most visible impact on query latency since specialists run in parallel and DB round-trips happen on the hot path for every request.\n",
